*This project has been created as part of the 42 curriculum by olmirosh.*

# ft_printf

## Description

ft_printf is a custom implementation of the standard C `printf` function.

The goal of the project is to understand variadic functions in C and to work with
`va_list`, `va_start`, `va_arg`, and `va_end`.

The function supports the following conversions:

- `%c` — character
- `%s` — string
- `%p` — pointer
- `%d` — signed decimal integer
- `%i` — signed integer
- `%u` — unsigned decimal integer
- `%x` — hexadecimal lowercase
- `%X` — hexadecimal uppercase
- `%%` — percent sign

The project produces a static library named:

```text
libftprintf.a
```

## Library Description

The main function is:

```c
int ft_printf(const char *format, ...);
```

The function reads the format string character by character.

Normal characters are written directly to standard output.

When a `%` character is found, the next character determines which argument type
must be read using `va_arg`.

The project contains helper functions for:

- printing characters;
- printing strings;
- printing signed integers;
- printing unsigned integers;
- printing hexadecimal numbers;
- printing pointer addresses.

Each printing function returns the number of characters written so that
`ft_printf` can return the total output length.

## Instructions

### Compilation

Run:

```bash
make
```

This creates:

```text
libftprintf.a
```

### Makefile commands

```bash
make
make clean
make fclean
make re
```

### Using the library

Include:

```c
#include "ft_printf.h"
```

Example:

```c
#include "ft_printf.h"

int main(void)
{
    ft_printf("Hello %s, number = %d\n", "world", 42);
    return (0);
}
```

Compile:

```bash
cc main.c libftprintf.a -o program
```

Run:

```bash
./program
```

## Implementation

The implementation uses C variadic arguments.

```c
va_list args;
va_start(args, format);
```

When a conversion is found, the corresponding value is read with `va_arg`.

For example:

```c
va_arg(args, int)
```

is used for `%d`, `%i`, and `%c`.

Hexadecimal values are printed recursively using a base string:

```text
0123456789abcdef
```

or:

```text
0123456789ABCDEF
```

Pointers are printed as hexadecimal addresses with the `0x` prefix.

Finally:

```c
va_end(args);
```

is used to finish processing the variadic arguments.

